      "math_academy_id": "q-340863",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}+4x-9$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$",
      "worked_solution": "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}+4(-x)-9) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-4x-9) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+4x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
